---
import Base from '@casoon/pages-theme/layouts/Base.astro';
import { url } from '@casoon/pages-theme/url';
import { outcomes, ruleIndex } from '../../lib/rules';

const { counts, rules, total } = ruleIndex;
const domainLabel = Object.fromEntries(counts.map((c) => [c.domain, c.label]));
const sum = (key: 'automatic' | 'hint_only' | 'manual') => counts.reduce((n, c) => n + c[key], 0);
---

<Base
  title="Rules"
  description="Every accessibility rule of auditmysite with its success criterion, outcome class and a passing and a failing example."
>
  <div class="page">
    <h1>Rules</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Every rule of the accessibility audit, and every security header check, structured-data
      feature and checked JavaScript library, has a page here: what it checks, how it is tested
      and, where the detection corpus has one, a failing and a passing example. The pages are
      generated from the rule catalogue and the rule contracts, so they cannot drift from what the
      tool does.
    </p>

    <h2>Coverage</h2>
    <table>
      <caption class="sr-only">Pages by family and outcome class</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Family</th>
          <th scope="col">Automatic</th>
          <th scope="col">Hint only</th>
          <th scope="col">Manual</th>
          <th scope="col">Total</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {counts.map((c) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{c.label}</th>
            <td>{c.automatic}</td>
            <td>{c.hint_only}</td>
            <td>{c.manual}</td>
            <td>{c.total}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">All</th>
          <td>{sum('automatic')}</td>
          <td>{sum('hint_only')}</td>
          <td>{sum('manual')}</td>
          <td>{total}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <dl class="classes">
      {(Object.keys(outcomes) as (keyof typeof outcomes)[]).map((key) => (
        <>
          <dt>{outcomes[key].label}</dt>
          <dd>{outcomes[key].meaning}</dd>
        </>
      ))}
    </dl>
    <p>
      The class is declared by the rule's contract (<code>automation</code> and
      <code>review_only</code>), not by what the corpus happens to show. A security page can stand
      for several header checks that surface as one finding.
    </p>

    <h2>Addresses</h2>
    <p>
      A rule's page is <code>rules/&lt;rule_id&gt;/</code> below this site, with the id unchanged:
      <code>/</code> stays a path separator, so <code>document/title-missing</code> is at
      <a href={url('rules/document/title-missing/')}><code>rules/document/title-missing/</code></a>.
      The <code>help_url</code> of a finding in the JSON, SARIF and AI report points here, using
      the id the finding carries: <code>security.headers.missing_header</code> and
      <code>best_practices.vulnerable_libraries.jquery</code> for the other families; structured-data
      findings (<code>seo.schema.schema_rule_missing_&lt;feature&gt;_&lt;property&gt;</code>) point at
      their feature's page, <code>seo.schema.schema_rule_missing_&lt;feature&gt;</code>. A WCAG
      criterion's W3C Understanding page is <code>understanding_url</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>All rules</h2>
    <table>
      <caption class="sr-only">All pages, WCAG rules by success criterion</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Family</th>
          <th scope="col">Criterion</th>
          <th scope="col">Level</th>
          <th scope="col">Rule</th>
          <th scope="col">Outcome class</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {rules.map((rule) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{domainLabel[rule.domain]}</td>
            <td>{rule.criterion ?? (rule.domain === 'wcag' ? 'Best practice' : '')}</td>
            <td>{rule.level ?? ''}</td>
            <td>
              <a href={url(`rules/${rule.id}/`)}>{rule.title}</a><br /><code>{rule.id}</code>
            </td>
            <td>{outcomes[rule.outcome].label}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: 960px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 56px 24px 96px;
  }
  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    color: var(--heading);
    font-size: 38px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }
  h2 {
    margin: 44px 0 12px;
    color: var(--heading);
    font-size: 22px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .lead {
    font-size: 17.5px;
  }
  table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 14.5px;
  }
  th,
  td {
    padding: 9px 12px 9px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  thead th {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  code {
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 5px;
    background: var(--code-bg);
    font-size: 0.86em;
  }
  a {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
  }
  .classes {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 120px 1fr;
    gap: 6px 24px;
    margin: 20px 0;
    font-size: 14.5px;
  }
  .classes dt {
    color: var(--heading);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .classes dd {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .sr-only {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
